cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Saudi Legal Corpus: verified legislation at article level, published
  judgments in full text, and a citator joining them
message: >-
  If you use this corpus or the tools built on it, please cite it. Each
  record carries its own provenance; the field notes in the repository say
  what the data will and will not support. The licence below covers the code
  and the annotations made here. It does not cover the underlying official
  texts: the legislation and the judgments are government publications, the
  binding Arabic original governs in all cases, and NOTICE.md sets out what
  this repository does and does not claim.
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Almohammedi
    given-names: Abdullah
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0001-0832-0995
    country: SA
abstract: >-
  Two corpora for one jurisdiction, joined at the level at which a lawyer
  argues. The legislative side holds 15,855 verified articles across 291
  Saudi instruments, each with its number, text, legal status and retrieval
  provenance. The judicial side holds 50,666 judgments published in full text
  by the Ministry of Justice, of which 15,383 carry the appellate decision as
  well. A citator links them: 99,158 statutory citations matched to the
  article cited, each carrying the segment of the judgment it sits in, whether
  the court or a party is speaking, and whether that judgment survived appeal.
keywords:
  - empirical legal studies
  - legislation
  - judicial citation
  - legal informatics
  - Arabic legal corpora
  - open government data
  - Saudi Arabia
license: MIT
repository-code: https://github.com/Abdullah-m7/saudi-legal-corpus-ai
